1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:14,001
On the shore and in the surf, in times of peace and in times of war, this is the story of beach technology.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It's a perfect day at the beach.

3
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:50,540
The sun, the sand, the surf, all working together to create a sensory feast.

4
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:58,700
But what we don't see are all the technologies that help us interact with this unique environment.

5
00:00:58,701 --> 00:01:02,700
The swimsuit we wear, the sunscreen we apply,

6
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,200
the cooler that holds our drinks,

7
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:08,700
even the surfboard that lets us skate around on the waves.

8
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,940
All these and so much more are made possible

9
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:16,760
by technological innovation.

10
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:24,700
But as for the natural stuff, the fresh ocean breezes,

11
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:27,700
the roar of the breakers, the feel of sand

12
00:01:27,701 --> 00:01:29,500
warmed by the sun.

13
00:01:29,501 --> 00:01:33,140
We know they'll never be a substitute for them, right?

14
00:01:33,141 --> 00:01:34,140
Think again.

15
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:37,680
Welcome to the Ocean Dome.

16
00:01:37,681 --> 00:01:39,980
With a capacity of 10,000 visitors,

17
00:01:39,981 --> 00:01:43,580
it is the world's largest indoor artificial beach.

18
00:01:43,581 --> 00:01:47,320
Completed in 1993, the Ocean Dome is part of a sprawling

19
00:01:47,321 --> 00:01:52,360
Japanese resort called Sigaia, located in Miyazaki, Japan.

20
00:01:52,361 --> 00:01:55,220
Though it lies a mere quarter mile from a real beach,

21
00:01:55,221 --> 00:01:58,220
the ocean dome is a totally fabricated environment.

22
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:03,320
Everything's very controlled,

23
00:02:03,420 --> 00:02:09,260
and what they are interacting with is really technology.

24
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:10,760
Instead of real sand,

25
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:14,280
its 30,000 square foot beach called Sugar Beach

26
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:18,140
is made up of over 600 tons of crushed white marble

27
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:19,820
imported from China.

28
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:23,340
The sand acts as a filter, letting the water seep through

29
00:02:23,341 --> 00:02:25,940
into a massive reservoir beneath.

30
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:28,080
But don't expect seawater in here.

31
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:30,540
The ocean dome's 2 million-plus gallons

32
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:35,080
are chlorinated, salt-free, and kept at a constant 86 degrees.

33
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,160
A really fine virtual resort.

34
00:02:45,260 --> 00:02:47,600
So you can always keep the water the right temperature.

35
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:51,900
You can have no waves or big waves.

36
00:02:51,901 --> 00:02:57,040
A computer-controlled system produces cookie-cutter waves big enough to surf on.

37
00:02:57,720 --> 00:03:05,540
Ten vacuum pumps pull the water into 20 large chambers, where it then gets released, creating up to eight-foot-high waves.

38
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:08,360
And there's never any worry about sunburn.

39
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:11,140
That is, until they open up the roof.

40
00:03:11,141 --> 00:03:23,360
Enormous motors drive apart the four arched panels of the 2,800-ton roof, which takes only 10 minutes to open completely.

41
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:27,660
They do everything with technology.

42
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:32,600
And I think that probably many people like the cleanliness of it.

43
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:36,060
Gentle breezes are produced by fans.

44
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:38,680
The sound of seagulls comes from a recording.

45
00:03:38,681 --> 00:03:41,880
But these predictable pleasures may not be everybody's idea

46
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:43,920
of the perfect day at the beach.

47
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:47,460
Still, Sigaya stands as the culmination of a beach

48
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:52,260
technology going back to ancient Rome.

49
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:54,200
At the height of Imperial Rome,

50
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:57,260
the Romans colonized the shores of the Mediterranean

51
00:03:57,360 --> 00:04:01,640
with sumptuous villas, with palaces and swimming pools,

52
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:04,700
which they built along the coast.

53
00:04:04,701 --> 00:04:10,480
So they were filled with tidal waters, always fresh water.

54
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:13,740
In the first century AD, the emperor Tiberius

55
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:17,880
built his villa in Spurlonga with an enormous cave-like grotto

56
00:04:17,980 --> 00:04:21,220
that had pools and fish ponds fed by the ocean.

57
00:04:21,320 --> 00:04:23,180
For the ancient Romans, the beach

58
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:25,620
was a place of unbridled luxury.

59
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:33,360
The beach culture of the Romans was very hedonistic.

60
00:04:33,361 --> 00:04:38,160
There were resorts where spectacular beach events

61
00:04:38,260 --> 00:04:39,600
were staged.

62
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:42,900
Swimming girls would cavort in pools.

63
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:50,380
People came to have affairs.

64
00:04:50,480 --> 00:04:53,380
When Rome would fall, the idea of the beach

65
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:55,880
as a happy place would fall with it.

66
00:04:55,881 --> 00:04:56,780
with it.

67
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:04,880
In the Middle Ages, many believed that exposure to water

68
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:06,820
would leave them vulnerable to the plague.

69
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:13,000
Then, in the 18th century, people

70
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:16,360
came to believe that a practice called medicinal dunking

71
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:19,500
could cure everything from depression to leprosy.

72
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:21,240
A person was carried out to the water

73
00:05:21,340 --> 00:05:24,780
in a strange contraption called a bathing machine.

74
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:30,880
It was a wooden cabin on wheels that was drawn into the surf

75
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:36,520
by a horse attended by a bather, a matron or a man.

76
00:05:36,620 --> 00:05:40,260
And the patient would get up into the bathing machine

77
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:44,360
and then emerge in the water and be dunked and held

78
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:49,340
under water for almost a minute and then brought up sputtering.

79
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:51,300
And you'd think their normal reaction

80
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:53,780
would be to escape immediately.

81
00:05:53,781 --> 00:05:56,020
and by swimming away.

82
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:58,360
The only problem was that at that time,

83
00:05:58,460 --> 00:06:02,060
hardly anyone knew how to swim.

84
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:04,960
To learn how to swim, people in the 19th century

85
00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:08,200
turned to technology, everything from a simple harness

86
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:11,440
to elaborate systems of plates and fins.

87
00:06:11,540 --> 00:06:16,240
As more people learned to swim, the more they visited the beach.

88
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:19,480
By the mid to late 1800s, the Industrial Revolution

89
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:22,480
swept America, and people were discovering the beach

90
00:06:22,481 --> 00:06:24,480
the beach in great numbers.

91
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:31,480
They would work horrendous hours,

92
00:06:31,481 --> 00:06:36,480
but they had a little block of time that they began to call their leisure time.

93
00:06:36,481 --> 00:06:42,480
And some entrepreneurs were very eager to fill that time for these people who had nothing to do.

94
00:06:42,481 --> 00:06:49,480
And it just happened that there was a group of developers who had discovered this forsaken island

95
00:06:49,481 --> 00:06:51,480
off the coast of New Jersey.

96
00:06:51,481 --> 00:06:54,580
which soon became Atlantic City.

97
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:02,220
A new railroad would soon deliver millions to the New Jersey beach town.

98
00:07:02,780 --> 00:07:04,200
Merchants quickly set up shop.

99
00:07:04,620 --> 00:07:09,140
In 1870, a legend appeared, America's first boardwalk.

100
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:15,040
The celebrated boardwalk was really a commercially motivated structure.

101
00:07:15,041 --> 00:07:18,040
The merchants didn't want sand in their establishments,

102
00:07:18,041 --> 00:07:22,880
and so they bankrolled the construction of the boardwalk.

103
00:07:22,980 --> 00:07:24,880
Made of planks of southern pine,

104
00:07:24,980 --> 00:07:28,480
Atlantic City's original boardwalk cost only $5,000

105
00:07:28,580 --> 00:07:31,980
and was only 10 feet wide.

106
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:34,920
But as the town grew, so did its boardwalk.

107
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:37,320
It was elevated higher and restructured

108
00:07:37,420 --> 00:07:40,560
to withstand the weight of hundreds of thousands.

109
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:42,800
By the early decades of the 20th century,

110
00:07:42,900 --> 00:07:46,160
it had grown into a cultural and commercial phenomenon.

111
00:07:46,161 --> 00:07:50,160
With its huge amusement piers and unique rolling chairs,

112
00:07:50,260 --> 00:07:53,900
it was the place to go and the place to be seen.

113
00:07:56,900 --> 00:08:01,500
Today, the boardwalk is up to 60 feet wide and four miles long.

114
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:04,920
Its surface is mostly made of a strong tropical hardwood

115
00:08:05,020 --> 00:08:07,820
called ipe and is permanently anchored

116
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:09,220
into a concrete substructure.

117
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:14,320
Atlantic City and its boardwalk

118
00:08:14,321 --> 00:08:17,920
are more popular than ever, as over 37 million tourists

119
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:20,560
annually pay a visit to the birthplace

120
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:22,560
of modern American beach culture.

121
00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:31,200
The total amount of lumber currently in the Atlantic City

122
00:08:31,300 --> 00:08:34,940
Boardwalk stands at roughly 7.5 million feet,

123
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:37,040
enough to build 800 homes.

124
00:08:41,220 --> 00:08:43,040
The modern beach is a destination

125
00:08:43,041 --> 00:08:44,840
for fun and sport.

126
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:47,340
But throughout history, the shore has also

127
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:50,980
been a significant gateway, a place for discovery.

128
00:08:51,080 --> 00:08:54,180
And nowhere was this more true than on the beaches

129
00:08:54,280 --> 00:08:55,180
of the New World.

130
00:08:58,780 --> 00:09:02,700
The beach for the explorers was a site of triumph.

131
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:08,740
Columbus first glimpsed the possibilities

132
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:13,040
of a new world on the shores of the New World.

133
00:09:13,041 --> 00:09:15,040
on the far reaches of the Caribbean

134
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:16,640
and planted his flags there.

135
00:09:20,540 --> 00:09:23,680
Columbus and others like him saw an alien world

136
00:09:23,780 --> 00:09:27,280
with exotic cultures and untold resources.

137
00:09:27,380 --> 00:09:30,820
After years of struggle, they conquered this world,

138
00:09:30,920 --> 00:09:33,000
then realized they had to defend it from those

139
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:34,900
who would take it from them.

140
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,100
To protect their growing colonies,

141
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:40,800
they built forts and outposts up and down the coast.

142
00:09:40,801 --> 00:09:44,900
One of the greatest was the massive Castillo de San Marcos,

143
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,040
the oldest fortress in America.

144
00:09:55,940 --> 00:09:59,520
The Spanish began building it in 1672 in what

145
00:09:59,620 --> 00:10:01,920
is now St. Augustine, Florida.

146
00:10:02,020 --> 00:10:04,900
When the castle was completed 23 years later,

147
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:06,900
it was an awesome accomplishment.

148
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,000
The 33-foot high walls were built of mortar and mortar

149
00:10:10,001 --> 00:10:13,240
and a local stone called coquina, which is made up

150
00:10:13,340 --> 00:10:15,740
of billions of fossilized sea creatures.

151
00:10:18,740 --> 00:10:22,640
Made of the ocean itself, the massive 12-foot thick walls

152
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:27,660
were able to absorb repeated volleys of enemy cannonballs.

153
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:31,720
In its 205-year history, the old castle on the beach

154
00:10:31,820 --> 00:10:35,300
never once fell in battle.

155
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:37,500
A fortress is built for two reasons.

156
00:10:37,501 --> 00:10:41,700
One is that it is there to actually defend against any

157
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:43,140
attack that might come on.

158
00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:45,940
On the other hand, you could also construct a fortification

159
00:10:46,040 --> 00:10:51,040
so that it would intimidate any enemy from ever attacking it.

160
00:10:51,140 --> 00:10:54,320
That was the idea behind the most extensive beach

161
00:10:54,420 --> 00:11:00,060
fortification in history, Hitler's Atlantic Wall.

162
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:03,920
After occupying France in 1940, the Nazis realized

163
00:11:04,020 --> 00:11:06,700
that the English Channel would be the likely route

164
00:11:06,701 --> 00:11:08,360
of Allied invasion.

165
00:11:08,460 --> 00:11:12,540
So Hitler set about creating a 1,200-mile-long coastal

166
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:17,340
defense system from Denmark to the Spanish-French border.

167
00:11:17,440 --> 00:11:20,040
It consisted really of two types of technology.

168
00:11:20,140 --> 00:11:24,180
One is the barrier to getting onto the beach,

169
00:11:24,280 --> 00:11:27,960
anti-tank or anti-ship structures that were placed

170
00:11:28,060 --> 00:11:30,700
in the sand and in the water offshore

171
00:11:30,800 --> 00:11:32,920
to make it very difficult to find a place where you could

172
00:11:33,020 --> 00:11:34,860
actually get a boat onto the beach.

173
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:40,700
The next barrier was the string of heavy artillery bunkers

174
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:44,700
with six-foot-thick concrete and steel-reinforced walls

175
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:46,840
that punctuated the cliffs.

176
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:50,220
But the Allies knew that in order to retake the continent,

177
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:53,720
the Atlantic Wall would have to be breached.

178
00:11:53,820 --> 00:11:56,760
Fortunately, General Eisenhower's D-Day landing

179
00:11:56,860 --> 00:11:59,960
troops employed some beach technology of their own.

180
00:12:02,060 --> 00:12:04,700
The bulk of the infantry came ashore

181
00:12:04,701 --> 00:12:09,100
came ashore in a very small boat called the Higgins boat,

182
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:12,500
which had a ramp on the front that formed a bow.

183
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:16,440
And as soon as they got in shallow enough water to ground

184
00:12:16,540 --> 00:12:19,780
out, they dropped the ramp, and everybody

185
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:21,180
starts running for the beach.

186
00:12:29,920 --> 00:12:31,520
There were other landing craft,

187
00:12:31,521 --> 00:12:36,260
but the Higgins boat, also called LCVP for landing craft

188
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:39,760
vehicle personnel, was the workhorse of the amphibious

189
00:12:39,860 --> 00:12:40,760
fleet.

190
00:12:40,860 --> 00:12:45,040
Designed by Andrew Higgins, this boat was 36 feet long

191
00:12:45,140 --> 00:12:48,680
and could carry 36 combat-equipped soldiers or four

192
00:12:48,780 --> 00:12:50,580
tons of cargo from ship to shore.

193
00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:58,140
Its 225 horsepower engine and shallow draft bottom,

194
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:00,480
flatter than a normal V-shaped hull,

195
00:13:00,481 --> 00:13:03,320
allowed the boat to deliver its cargo on the beach

196
00:13:03,420 --> 00:13:05,660
and then quickly retract.

197
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:09,060
This ability to get in and out was crucial to an operation

198
00:13:09,160 --> 00:13:11,620
as massive as the D-Day landing, which

199
00:13:11,720 --> 00:13:14,360
was the largest amphibious landing in history.

200
00:13:17,700 --> 00:13:19,960
Years later, President Eisenhower

201
00:13:20,060 --> 00:13:25,800
would call Andrew Higgins the man who won the war for us.

202
00:13:25,900 --> 00:13:27,780
Maybe the Higgins boat won that war,

203
00:13:27,781 --> 00:13:30,480
but the Navy hasn't stopped trying to improve on it.

204
00:13:34,480 --> 00:13:37,620
The current state of the art is the LCAP,

205
00:13:37,720 --> 00:13:41,320
or landing craft air cushion.

206
00:13:41,420 --> 00:13:46,840
Introduced in 1987, its hull never touches water or land.

207
00:13:46,940 --> 00:13:49,540
It is a classic hovercraft.

208
00:13:49,640 --> 00:13:52,640
This means that it travels over water and over land

209
00:13:52,740 --> 00:13:54,040
on a cushion of air.

210
00:13:54,041 --> 00:13:57,380
The craft is supported by a full rubber skirt.

211
00:13:57,480 --> 00:14:01,820
There are lift fans on board which push air into the skirt

212
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:04,820
and thereby keeping the craft up on cushion.

213
00:14:04,920 --> 00:14:08,720
Four gas turbine engines capable of pumping 16,000

214
00:14:08,820 --> 00:14:13,660
horsepower lift and propel the vehicle by forced air alone.

215
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:17,500
Carrying a 60-ton payload, 15 times the capability

216
00:14:17,600 --> 00:14:20,940
of the Higgins boat, she can reach 70% of the world's

217
00:14:20,941 --> 00:14:25,180
traveling at speeds of 50-plus miles an hour.

218
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:27,580
At these freeway speeds, he can come up to the beach,

219
00:14:27,680 --> 00:14:29,180
does not have to stop at the beach,

220
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:33,680
but can transverse across into firmer ground, less exposed.

221
00:14:36,280 --> 00:14:38,560
The military looks for tactical advantage

222
00:14:38,660 --> 00:14:40,400
in designing beach vehicles.

223
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:43,400
For civilians, the name of the game is fun.

224
00:14:43,401 --> 00:14:44,300
his father.

225
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,600
Bruce Myers didn't invent the dune buggy.

226
00:14:52,700 --> 00:14:56,040
He just made it more enjoyable and accessible.

227
00:14:56,140 --> 00:14:58,540
He was the Henry Ford of dune buggies,

228
00:14:58,640 --> 00:15:02,620
and his Model T was the Myers Manx.

229
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:06,260
In 1964, the first Manx hit the beach.

230
00:15:06,360 --> 00:15:10,200
Myers' whimsical design called for a 1960s Volkswagen

231
00:15:10,201 --> 00:15:13,040
as its base with one key difference.

232
00:15:13,140 --> 00:15:16,600
The lower chassis or floor pan would have to be shortened

233
00:15:16,700 --> 00:15:18,840
by about 14 inches.

234
00:15:18,940 --> 00:15:21,380
This made the buggy stouter and stronger

235
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:23,320
with a tighter turning radius.

236
00:15:23,420 --> 00:15:26,880
But the crowning glory was the molded fiberglass body

237
00:15:26,980 --> 00:15:27,880
that went on top.

238
00:15:30,820 --> 00:15:33,820
This dropped the weight to a sporty 1,200 pounds

239
00:15:33,920 --> 00:15:37,300
and gave the Manx its distinctive look.

240
00:15:37,301 --> 00:15:39,800
But you couldn't buy the Manx in a store.

241
00:15:39,900 --> 00:15:42,340
If you came up with the VW lower half,

242
00:15:42,440 --> 00:15:45,140
Myers would sell you a $400 kit that

243
00:15:45,240 --> 00:15:48,680
included the upper half, the dashboard, hood, and body,

244
00:15:48,780 --> 00:15:52,180
with explicit directions on putting it all together.

245
00:15:52,280 --> 00:15:56,060
He sold over 6,000 of the kits and helped launch a national

246
00:15:56,160 --> 00:15:58,120
craze for off-road vehicles.

247
00:16:03,560 --> 00:16:05,520
To cash in on the dune buggy craze,

248
00:16:05,521 --> 00:16:07,520
manufacturers produced over a quarter million

249
00:16:07,521 --> 00:16:09,020
Myers-Manks lookalikes.

250
00:16:09,021 --> 00:16:12,520
Today, an original Manx can fetch over $100,000.

251
00:16:23,540 --> 00:16:26,280
San Francisco, 1896.

252
00:16:26,281 --> 00:16:31,020
Wealthy industrialist Adolph Sutro takes the beach inside.

253
00:16:31,021 --> 00:16:33,840
Built right on the city's rugged western shore,

254
00:16:33,841 --> 00:16:38,140
historic Sutro Baths was the largest indoor swimming pool

255
00:16:38,240 --> 00:16:38,940
ever built.

256
00:16:43,040 --> 00:16:47,380
It was enclosed in an enormous glass and steel structure.

257
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:49,580
So you could see the weather outside.

258
00:16:49,680 --> 00:16:53,500
It was, in a way, a beach packed into a building.

259
00:16:56,100 --> 00:16:59,200
Sutro filled his pools with fresh seawater that

260
00:16:59,300 --> 00:17:02,800
was channeled in at high tide and then fed into six swimming

261
00:17:02,801 --> 00:17:07,480
to six swimming pools, which held over 1.8 million gallons.

262
00:17:07,481 --> 00:17:12,720
Incredibly, it took only an hour to fill all the tanks.

263
00:17:12,721 --> 00:17:14,860
Along the way, the water was heated

264
00:17:14,861 --> 00:17:18,020
to various temperatures for the different pools.

265
00:17:18,021 --> 00:17:20,920
The size of this was absolutely enormous.

266
00:17:20,921 --> 00:17:24,700
The whole sequence of structures filled about three acres

267
00:17:24,701 --> 00:17:29,840
and could accommodate up to 20,000 visitors in a single day.

268
00:17:29,841 --> 00:17:32,140
And if those visitors needed a swimsuit,

269
00:17:32,141 --> 00:17:36,100
They could rent one of the 20,000 the baths had available to the public.

270
00:17:36,740 --> 00:17:41,420
The simple black jersey one piece was far different from what swimwear had been

271
00:17:41,421 --> 00:17:43,960
and what it was about to become.

272
00:17:51,860 --> 00:17:54,780
In the beginning, swimwear was not for swimming.

273
00:17:55,400 --> 00:17:56,520
It was for modesty.

274
00:17:56,521 --> 00:17:59,520
A flash of a woman's leg was taboo,

275
00:17:59,620 --> 00:18:01,160
and one would go to great lengths

276
00:18:01,260 --> 00:18:03,760
to keep her bathing dress down in the water.

277
00:18:06,700 --> 00:18:10,060
They wore weights in the hems of their garments.

278
00:18:10,160 --> 00:18:14,800
They had to prevent themselves from being seen.

279
00:18:14,900 --> 00:18:16,840
By the turn of the 20th century,

280
00:18:16,940 --> 00:18:20,240
heavy knee-length wool dresses with bloomers and stockings

281
00:18:20,340 --> 00:18:21,540
were standard.

282
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:25,520
But in 1907, scandal erupted when a world champion swimmer

283
00:18:25,521 --> 00:18:28,160
a world champion swimmer named Annette Kellerman

284
00:18:28,161 --> 00:18:32,720
wore a tight-fitting one-piece suit that exposed her legs.

285
00:18:32,721 --> 00:18:36,440
She pioneered the first functional swimsuit

286
00:18:36,441 --> 00:18:39,060
that is not a bathing costume, but an actual costume

287
00:18:39,061 --> 00:18:41,900
in which one could move through water.

288
00:18:41,901 --> 00:18:43,640
For this liberating act,

289
00:18:43,641 --> 00:18:46,400
she was arrested for public indecency,

290
00:18:46,401 --> 00:18:48,040
as were many of her followers.

291
00:18:50,580 --> 00:18:52,240
But there was no turning back.

292
00:18:55,520 --> 00:19:02,400
And suddenly, manufacturers were on a search for the perfect fabric that could be both fashionable and practical.

293
00:19:04,560 --> 00:19:08,560
In the 20s, it was tightly knit, long-fibred wool.

294
00:19:09,700 --> 00:19:18,440
In the 30s, rubberized fabric called lastex allowed for suits that fit like a second skin, but still covered enough to be modest.

295
00:19:19,780 --> 00:19:23,100
Then, after World War II, a bombshell.

296
00:19:25,520 --> 00:19:31,540
Named after the Bikini Atoll, an atomic test site in the Pacific Islands,

297
00:19:31,900 --> 00:19:36,820
the naval-bearing Bikini shocked everyone when it was introduced in 1946.

298
00:19:39,640 --> 00:19:43,500
So much that it wasn't until the youth explosion of the 60s

299
00:19:43,501 --> 00:19:47,280
that those barely visible swimsuits were popping up everywhere.

300
00:19:47,281 --> 00:19:57,960
The 70s and 80s saw plenty of these revealing suits, many made from a newer synthetic called

301
00:19:57,961 --> 00:19:59,579
lycra, an elastic fiber.

302
00:19:59,580 --> 00:20:04,100
Lycra can stretch up to five times its original shape and still fit

303
00:20:04,101 --> 00:20:13,320
perfectly. The latest in a long line of so-called miracle fabrics is microfiber,

304
00:20:13,321 --> 00:20:19,940
100 times smaller than a human hair, microfiber resembles a pie in cross-section,

305
00:20:20,320 --> 00:20:22,920
with many finer fibers along its entire length.

306
00:20:23,540 --> 00:20:29,160
Strong and light, it's a far cry from the heavy wool swimsuits of 100 years ago.

307
00:20:30,580 --> 00:20:37,440
But with advances in technology, the strongest protection on the beach today is not a swimsuit at all.

308
00:20:37,441 --> 00:20:44,880
The chemicals in sunscreen now allow us to expose more skin than ever, while still protecting

309
00:20:44,881 --> 00:20:49,120
us from the sun's cancer-causing ultraviolet rays.

310
00:20:49,121 --> 00:20:55,200
Beginning in the 20s, scientists made preparations of zinc oxide and titanium dioxide, whose

311
00:20:55,201 --> 00:21:03,560
particles reflect ultraviolet rays.

312
00:21:03,561 --> 00:21:08,560
In the first year, Paba, short for para-aminobenzoic acid,

313
00:21:08,660 --> 00:21:10,900
was the first organic sunscreen.

314
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,200
Unfortunately, it would stain clothing,

315
00:21:13,300 --> 00:21:15,980
and many people were allergic to its chemical makeup.

316
00:21:16,080 --> 00:21:19,240
So today, most sunscreens are Paba-free.

317
00:21:19,340 --> 00:21:23,080
These modern sunscreens actually absorb UV radiation

318
00:21:23,180 --> 00:21:26,020
in much the same way the darker skin pigment does,

319
00:21:26,120 --> 00:21:28,420
by converting these UV waves to heat

320
00:21:28,520 --> 00:21:30,580
and then dissipating them harmlessly.

321
00:21:30,581 --> 00:21:33,720
Sun protection is a must for anyone at the beach,

322
00:21:33,721 --> 00:21:37,720
even if you're in the water waiting for waves of another kind.

323
00:21:42,220 --> 00:21:46,660
Modern surfing has roots that go back hundreds of years in Hawaii.

324
00:21:46,661 --> 00:21:51,200
And up until the 20s and 30s, many surfboards were handmade of redwood,

325
00:21:51,201 --> 00:21:55,800
up to 15 feet long, and could weigh up to 200 pounds.

326
00:21:55,801 --> 00:22:02,680
You know, 200-pound boards were the Redwood days, which was before my dad.

327
00:22:03,260 --> 00:22:06,860
My dad was started with balsa, and balsa's actually pretty light.

328
00:22:08,460 --> 00:22:16,700
In 1950, Hobie Alter, a surfer himself, began making balsa surfboards in his garage in Laguna Beach, California.

329
00:22:17,200 --> 00:22:22,660
He would laboriously glue the wood together and then shape it with a router that he mounted on a jake.

330
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:25,160
Then he would plane it.

331
00:22:25,800 --> 00:22:30,800
sand it, and then have it glassed,

332
00:22:30,801 --> 00:22:34,800
a messy process that sealed the board with fiberglass resin.

333
00:22:36,800 --> 00:22:39,800
For seven years, he made surfboards this way,

334
00:22:39,801 --> 00:22:41,800
completing only around a dozen a week.

335
00:22:41,801 --> 00:22:45,800
But space-age technology was about to change everything.

336
00:22:47,800 --> 00:22:52,800
In 1957, a material called polyurethane foam

337
00:22:52,801 --> 00:22:56,800
would transform Hobie's shop and an industry.

338
00:22:58,800 --> 00:23:01,800
The difference there would be you could mass produce boards.

339
00:23:02,800 --> 00:23:07,800
Foam was like shaping a stick of butter compared to a piece of solid wood.

340
00:23:08,800 --> 00:23:14,800
Today, it all begins with a foam blank, which is quickly roughed out by hand and then prepared for glassing.

341
00:23:14,801 --> 00:23:16,378
prepared for glassing.

342
00:23:16,379 --> 00:23:21,040
But when demand is high, a computerized shaping machine, a direct

343
00:23:21,041 --> 00:23:26,520
descendant of Hobie's old router on a jig, takes care of business two at a time. The

344
00:23:26,521 --> 00:23:32,460
exact measurements are programmed in and the foam flies in fractions of an inch.

345
00:23:32,461 --> 00:23:39,460
It takes a large mass of foam and brings it down to a level where I can finish it off

346
00:23:39,461 --> 00:23:40,161
by hand.

347
00:23:40,162 --> 00:23:44,700
Even if it's rough by me or if it's rough by the machine, someone still has to

348
00:23:44,701 --> 00:23:46,940
come along and fine tune the board.

349
00:23:46,941 --> 00:23:48,980
From that point it goes to the glasser.

350
00:23:48,981 --> 00:23:52,300
At the glassing shop, pigment is mixed with resin

351
00:23:52,301 --> 00:23:53,980
and the glasser goes to work.

352
00:23:55,940 --> 00:23:58,940
What they basically do is they lay a board out,

353
00:23:58,941 --> 00:24:02,820
deck down, cut fiberglass cloth over the entire board,

354
00:24:02,821 --> 00:24:04,660
cut it out to the basic shape of the board

355
00:24:04,661 --> 00:24:07,980
with a little bit of an overlap, maybe two inches,

356
00:24:07,981 --> 00:24:10,620
pour catalyzed resin on top of it

357
00:24:10,621 --> 00:24:13,020
and squeegee it out onto the board

358
00:24:13,021 --> 00:24:17,260
and make a nice tight bond between your foam

359
00:24:17,261 --> 00:24:19,760
and the fiberglass cloth.

360
00:24:19,761 --> 00:24:22,140
And then they do the same process on the top with two

361
00:24:22,141 --> 00:24:23,500
layers, and then from there it goes

362
00:24:23,501 --> 00:24:26,260
on to putting a sanding coat of resin on.

363
00:24:29,740 --> 00:24:32,300
Then it's sanded, glossed, and then

364
00:24:32,301 --> 00:24:35,140
buffed to a high sheen, ready for the weight.

365
00:24:39,740 --> 00:24:41,780
There's a lot of boards going through the process,

366
00:24:41,781 --> 00:24:45,180
but every single board means something to somebody.

367
00:24:45,181 --> 00:24:46,580
And it's kind of neat that every board

368
00:24:46,581 --> 00:24:48,820
is just bring someone some little bit of happiness

369
00:24:48,821 --> 00:24:49,860
and they go out and try it.

370
00:24:49,861 --> 00:24:52,460
And it's just, you know, continues the cycle.

371
00:24:58,620 --> 00:25:01,000
In the 1950s, a mosaic was found

372
00:25:01,001 --> 00:25:04,240
in a fourth century Roman villa depicting several young women

373
00:25:04,241 --> 00:25:06,700
in skimpy two-piece recreational garments,

374
00:25:06,701 --> 00:25:08,640
very similar to the modern bikini.

375
00:25:11,780 --> 00:25:16,780
The sun is high, the waves are sparkling,

376
00:25:16,880 --> 00:25:19,280
and your eyes are killing you.

377
00:25:19,380 --> 00:25:23,020
You need sunglasses, but not any pair will do.

378
00:25:23,120 --> 00:25:26,020
Simple darkened lenses to cut out excess light

379
00:25:26,120 --> 00:25:28,360
have been around since the 18th century,

380
00:25:28,460 --> 00:25:31,000
but they did nothing to keep out the glare

381
00:25:31,100 --> 00:25:33,140
from harsh reflected light.

382
00:25:37,240 --> 00:25:39,700
By the mid 20th century, polarized lenses

383
00:25:39,701 --> 00:25:44,700
polarized lenses were a sight for sore eyes.

384
00:25:44,800 --> 00:25:46,900
Before inventing instant photography,

385
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:50,340
Edwin Land patented a cellophane-like polarizing

386
00:25:50,440 --> 00:25:54,120
filter in 1929, which made it possible to block rays

387
00:25:54,220 --> 00:25:58,080
of polarized light or light waves traveling in one direction.

388
00:25:58,180 --> 00:26:00,960
Today, microscopic crystals are embedded

389
00:26:01,060 --> 00:26:02,360
between layers of glass.

390
00:26:04,460 --> 00:26:07,320
The alignment of crystals are in one direction

391
00:26:07,321 --> 00:26:10,320
direction that blocks polarized light.

392
00:26:10,321 --> 00:26:14,320
And when you have reflected light, it's strongly polarized.

393
00:26:14,321 --> 00:26:16,320
Meaning it's traveling in a single plane.

394
00:26:16,321 --> 00:26:21,320
And when you have a single plane of light reach the polarized lens,

395
00:26:21,321 --> 00:26:25,320
which has the crystals lined up in a way to block that plane of light,

396
00:26:25,321 --> 00:26:28,320
that plane doesn't go through the lens.

397
00:26:28,321 --> 00:26:32,320
Sunglasses let you stay in the sun a little longer.

398
00:26:32,321 --> 00:26:34,320
But if you want to stay in the water longer,

399
00:26:34,321 --> 00:26:36,320
you'd better have a wetsuit.

400
00:26:37,320 --> 00:26:49,080
In the 1950s, a diehard surfer by the name of Jack O'Neill regularly plunged into the

401
00:26:49,081 --> 00:26:52,100
bone-chilling 40-degree water of Northern California.

402
00:26:52,800 --> 00:26:57,460
Nobody could last long at those temperatures, so O'Neill began stitching together suits

403
00:26:57,461 --> 00:27:02,160
made of neoprene, a synthetic rubber foam used in car door seals.

404
00:27:02,161 --> 00:27:07,660
The idea was to insulate the wearer's core body temperature.

405
00:27:08,260 --> 00:27:12,520
A little water getting in was no problem as the wearer's body heat would warm it up

406
00:27:12,521 --> 00:27:15,880
or the wetsuit would eventually expel it because of the tight fit.

407
00:27:21,100 --> 00:27:26,460
Along the way, O'Neill also invented the comical-looking dry suit called the Super Suit.

408
00:27:27,120 --> 00:27:30,960
Originally designed for at-sea rescues during hot air ballooning emergencies,

409
00:27:30,961 --> 00:27:35,620
the suit inflates with insulating air and becomes an instant life result.

410
00:27:38,140 --> 00:27:42,740
But it was the neoprene wetsuit that made it truly an endless summer.

411
00:27:43,300 --> 00:27:48,480
Now, people could withstand temperatures as low as 40 degrees for up to eight hours.

412
00:27:48,960 --> 00:27:52,040
When you're a kid, you can stay in the water a long time,

413
00:27:52,260 --> 00:27:56,580
and so the wetsuits definitely help stay out there as long as you could

414
00:27:56,581 --> 00:27:58,120
until your mom yelled at you to come in.

415
00:28:00,960 --> 00:28:06,500
If wetsuits have the ability to keep the cold from the warm, then coolers do just the opposite.

416
00:28:07,060 --> 00:28:09,840
The key, as in wetsuits, is insulation.

417
00:28:11,800 --> 00:28:16,220
Advances in plastics during World War II gave us polystyrene,

418
00:28:16,660 --> 00:28:22,420
a versatile plastic resin that can be easily expanded into a lightweight yet strong foam.

419
00:28:25,660 --> 00:28:29,300
Everybody calls it styrofoam, although that's not accurate.

420
00:28:29,301 --> 00:28:34,300
Technically, Styrofoam is only made by the Dow Chemical Company, which invented it,

421
00:28:34,301 --> 00:28:38,300
and never made coolers or any other kind of containers with it.

422
00:28:38,301 --> 00:28:42,300
Anyway, expanded polystyrene foam is a closed cell foam,

423
00:28:42,301 --> 00:28:46,300
meaning that it's made up of many little beads with trapped air inside.

424
00:28:46,301 --> 00:28:52,300
In fact, it's over 95% air, yet it's watertight and airtight enough to keep your drinks cold.

425
00:28:52,301 --> 00:29:01,340
And the next step in cooler technology?

426
00:29:02,100 --> 00:29:04,340
How about the can that cools itself?

427
00:29:05,020 --> 00:29:07,260
This is the instant cool can,

428
00:29:07,700 --> 00:29:12,640
capable of lowering the temperature of a beverage 30 degrees in just three minutes.

429
00:29:15,580 --> 00:29:18,980
Simply twist the bottom to activate the cooling process.

430
00:29:18,981 --> 00:29:23,080
A desiccant or moisture remover contained within a vacuum

431
00:29:23,081 --> 00:29:25,780
draws the heat from the beverage through an evaporator

432
00:29:25,781 --> 00:29:28,920
and then into an insulated heat sink container.

433
00:29:29,500 --> 00:29:33,180
It's non-toxic and recyclable, but so far no match

434
00:29:33,181 --> 00:29:36,660
for the popularity of expanded polystyrene foam,

435
00:29:37,240 --> 00:29:39,180
which seems to be everywhere on the beach,

436
00:29:39,700 --> 00:29:42,720
much to the dismay of people who have to clean up after it

437
00:29:42,721 --> 00:29:44,360
when it's not disposed of properly.

438
00:29:46,300 --> 00:29:48,400
It breaks up into a million pieces,

439
00:29:48,401 --> 00:29:50,400
and it's a maintenance nightmare.

440
00:29:50,500 --> 00:29:54,400
The wind comes up and blows all over the beach sand.

441
00:29:54,500 --> 00:29:56,400
It's not good for the environment.

442
00:29:56,500 --> 00:30:00,040
So it has an adverse impact all the way around.

443
00:30:00,140 --> 00:30:04,040
Foam products and every other possible kind of debris

444
00:30:04,140 --> 00:30:07,280
make beach maintenance along the urbanized coast of Los

445
00:30:07,380 --> 00:30:10,920
Angeles County a huge challenge.

446
00:30:11,020 --> 00:30:13,900
Of the 26 miles of beach that we operate and maintain,

447
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:16,300
we have 60 million people that come down to the beach,

448
00:30:16,301 --> 00:30:18,700
which equates about 4,800 tons of trash

449
00:30:18,800 --> 00:30:22,240
that we remove off the beach per calendar year.

450
00:30:22,340 --> 00:30:26,040
That can mean up to 16 tons of debris every day

451
00:30:26,140 --> 00:30:28,440
during the summer season.

452
00:30:28,540 --> 00:30:31,540
Beach technology to the rescue.

453
00:30:31,640 --> 00:30:36,460
To maintain our beaches, we have over 160 pieces of equipment.

454
00:30:36,560 --> 00:30:38,360
The agricultural tractors that we use

455
00:30:38,460 --> 00:30:41,900
are the same type of tractors that they use in the Midwest.

456
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:44,060
With one difference, the tires have

457
00:30:44,061 --> 00:30:47,300
have to be filled with over 100 pounds of water

458
00:30:47,400 --> 00:30:49,860
for extra traction on the sand.

459
00:30:49,960 --> 00:30:52,760
Then the four-wheel drive 16-gear tractor

460
00:30:52,860 --> 00:30:56,980
can tow a variety of grooming attachments.

461
00:30:57,080 --> 00:31:00,440
The barber surf ray collects the top layer of sand

462
00:31:00,540 --> 00:31:03,620
and flips the debris onto the tines of a conveyor belt,

463
00:31:03,720 --> 00:31:06,020
which then deposits it into a large hopper.

464
00:31:09,080 --> 00:31:12,720
But the beach sanitizer is the backbone of the fleet.

465
00:31:12,721 --> 00:31:15,320
It will pick up everything from cigarette filters

466
00:31:15,420 --> 00:31:18,320
to broken glass.

467
00:31:18,420 --> 00:31:20,260
And the unique thing about this piece of equipment

468
00:31:20,360 --> 00:31:22,760
is it's got a paddle wheel up in front here.

469
00:31:22,860 --> 00:31:25,460
And what that does, it takes the material on the beach

470
00:31:25,560 --> 00:31:27,960
and it throws the sand as well as the debris

471
00:31:28,060 --> 00:31:29,780
up on this wired mesh.

472
00:31:29,880 --> 00:31:32,880
What happens is that the sand will percolate

473
00:31:32,980 --> 00:31:36,080
through this wired mesh, goes back down to the beach,

474
00:31:36,180 --> 00:31:39,320
while all the debris and material will go up the wired

475
00:31:39,420 --> 00:31:42,480
mesh and into what we call the hopper, which is a big bucket.

476
00:31:42,481 --> 00:31:45,880
What we never ever want to do is to take natural sand,

477
00:31:45,980 --> 00:31:48,080
our beach sand that's so precious to us,

478
00:31:48,180 --> 00:31:50,180
off the beach and to a landfill.

479
00:31:50,380 --> 00:31:53,060
But more importantly, we want to take trash to the landfill

480
00:31:53,061 --> 00:31:55,860
and keep our sand here along the coastline of California.

481
00:31:58,200 --> 00:31:59,700
So much for the trash.

482
00:32:00,140 --> 00:32:03,200
But what about the things of value that are left on the beach?

483
00:32:03,620 --> 00:32:07,480
If you're unlucky enough to lose an expensive gold watch in the sand,

484
00:32:07,840 --> 00:32:10,360
you might want to invest in a metal detector.

485
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:15,980
In 1881, as President James Garfield

486
00:32:16,080 --> 00:32:18,160
laid dying from an assassin's bullet

487
00:32:18,260 --> 00:32:21,360
that his doctors couldn't find, no less an inventor

488
00:32:21,460 --> 00:32:24,500
than Alexander Graham Bell was summoned.

489
00:32:24,600 --> 00:32:27,100
He had developed a primitive metal detector

490
00:32:27,200 --> 00:32:29,800
that would make a sound when a metal object passed

491
00:32:29,900 --> 00:32:32,060
between two electromagnets.

492
00:32:32,160 --> 00:32:34,660
But the bullet was so deep, Bell's device

493
00:32:34,760 --> 00:32:38,140
couldn't get a reading, and Garfield eventually succumbed.

494
00:32:42,480 --> 00:32:46,320
The technology is still so simple that anyone can build a

495
00:32:46,420 --> 00:32:49,820
basic metal detector from a kit for less than $10.

496
00:32:49,920 --> 00:32:53,120
From there, they can get considerably more complex,

497
00:32:53,220 --> 00:32:57,220
but the idea remains the same.

498
00:32:57,320 --> 00:32:58,800
When you turn on your metal detector,

499
00:32:58,900 --> 00:33:02,200
you set up a field, and that field comes off of the loop

500
00:33:02,300 --> 00:33:04,040
that's at the end of your rod.

501
00:33:04,140 --> 00:33:09,780
Well, it is in a cone shape, and it goes down into the sand.

502
00:33:09,781 --> 00:33:14,780
And when you have a piece of metal unbalance that field,

503
00:33:14,880 --> 00:33:18,780
the machine picks it up and sends a signal to your earphone.

504
00:33:18,880 --> 00:33:20,320
It goes beep, and they say, hey,

505
00:33:20,420 --> 00:33:23,320
there's a piece of metal down there, and we need to dig it.

506
00:33:27,340 --> 00:33:29,140
Lance has done a lot of digging

507
00:33:29,240 --> 00:33:31,540
in his 22 years of metal detecting.

508
00:33:31,640 --> 00:33:35,540
And that's added up to thousands in silver and gold.

509
00:33:35,640 --> 00:33:38,540
But this treasure isn't the result of luck.

510
00:33:38,541 --> 00:33:43,540
Lance gets up well before dawn to do research on the computer.

511
00:33:43,541 --> 00:33:47,540
I look at the information from the harvest buoy

512
00:33:47,541 --> 00:33:51,540
and the deep water swell on the internet from Scripps,

513
00:33:51,541 --> 00:33:57,540
and it tells me what the degree of the swell is and the intensity.

514
00:33:57,541 --> 00:34:02,540
By studying the live reports from the Scripps Institution of Oceanography's website,

515
00:34:02,541 --> 00:34:07,540
Lance knows exactly when a particular beach will get the most erosion from waves,

516
00:34:07,541 --> 00:34:09,540
exposing valuable metals.

517
00:34:09,541 --> 00:34:12,040
Then he goes to work with the latest weapon

518
00:34:12,041 --> 00:34:16,540
in the metal detecting arsenal, pulse induction technology.

519
00:34:17,540 --> 00:34:21,540
Pulse induction today is the thing that's happening.

520
00:34:21,541 --> 00:34:22,540
It's the best.

521
00:34:23,540 --> 00:34:27,540
Pulse induction works by sending as many as 100 pulses

522
00:34:27,541 --> 00:34:30,540
of current per second into the ground.

523
00:34:30,541 --> 00:34:33,540
These pulses magnetize metallic objects

524
00:34:33,541 --> 00:34:36,540
up to 14 inches deep, which are then sensed

525
00:34:36,541 --> 00:34:37,800
by the machine.

526
00:34:37,900 --> 00:34:40,740
The technology works equally as well on dry land

527
00:34:40,840 --> 00:34:43,880
or in the surf.

528
00:34:43,980 --> 00:34:47,580
After you dug a bunch of holes and they're all bottle caps

529
00:34:47,680 --> 00:34:51,280
and wires, you learn to differentiate the sounds.

530
00:34:51,380 --> 00:34:55,000
And like a wire or a nail is a double beep.

531
00:34:55,100 --> 00:34:57,100
One way you pass your loop over it.

532
00:34:57,200 --> 00:34:59,100
And so you say, oh, I don't want to dig that one.

533
00:34:59,200 --> 00:35:00,500
I know what that is.

534
00:35:00,600 --> 00:35:05,340
But a nickel and a gold ring sound exactly the same

535
00:35:05,341 --> 00:35:06,840
in the sand.

536
00:35:06,940 --> 00:35:09,580
And when I hear a nickel, I really get excited.

537
00:35:09,680 --> 00:35:11,740
I think, oh, man, I got a chance for a gold ring.

538
00:35:11,840 --> 00:35:16,320
And you get it in your scoop, and you're starting

539
00:35:16,420 --> 00:35:19,420
to shake the sand out, and you feel the weight of whatever

540
00:35:19,520 --> 00:35:21,020
the target is in the basket.

541
00:35:21,120 --> 00:35:24,160
And the gold rings, they feel pretty good.

542
00:35:24,260 --> 00:35:27,100
They go clunk, clunk, clunk, and yeah, oh, all right.

543
00:35:27,200 --> 00:35:28,100
That's a good one.

544
00:35:31,440 --> 00:35:33,700
With up to 90,000 hits every day,

545
00:35:33,701 --> 00:35:37,200
the Scripps Institution of Oceanography's CDIP website

546
00:35:37,300 --> 00:35:39,940
is one of the most popular academic sites in the world.

547
00:35:48,220 --> 00:35:50,180
Even though they're mostly made of rock,

548
00:35:50,280 --> 00:35:53,920
beaches are fluid things, subject to the same waxing

549
00:35:54,020 --> 00:35:58,360
and waning as the tides that nourish them.

550
00:35:58,460 --> 00:36:00,720
In most beaches, the large waves of winter

551
00:36:00,721 --> 00:36:02,220
will scour a beach.

552
00:36:02,320 --> 00:36:05,360
And most beaches during the calmer summer months,

553
00:36:05,460 --> 00:36:06,560
the sand builds back up.

554
00:36:06,660 --> 00:36:07,860
That's a natural cycle.

555
00:36:11,860 --> 00:36:14,560
But sometimes the sea subtracts from beaches

556
00:36:14,660 --> 00:36:16,060
more than it adds.

557
00:36:16,160 --> 00:36:17,940
It's called beach erosion.

558
00:36:18,040 --> 00:36:19,880
And when it threatens our shorelines,

559
00:36:19,980 --> 00:36:23,980
agencies like the US Army Corps of Engineers go to work.

560
00:36:24,080 --> 00:36:26,880
They've been at the forefront of beach technology

561
00:36:26,980 --> 00:36:30,720
since their establishment as a permanent agency in 1880.

562
00:36:30,721 --> 00:36:34,320
One of their modern missions is beach replenishment,

563
00:36:34,420 --> 00:36:36,820
which can turn a narrow strip of beach

564
00:36:36,920 --> 00:36:40,760
into one that looks like this.

565
00:36:40,860 --> 00:36:43,200
The beach replenishment jobs and the maintenance

566
00:36:43,300 --> 00:36:46,240
stretching jobs we do places large quantities of sand

567
00:36:46,340 --> 00:36:47,300
on the beach.

568
00:36:47,400 --> 00:36:49,840
And without that sand, a lot of the beaches

569
00:36:49,940 --> 00:36:52,640
that we take for granted or that are there that people

570
00:36:52,740 --> 00:36:55,280
recreate on would just not be there.

571
00:36:55,380 --> 00:36:58,380
In 2001, in Redondo Beach, California,

572
00:36:58,381 --> 00:37:01,120
California, the Army Corps of Engineers,

573
00:37:01,121 --> 00:37:02,880
with the help of local government,

574
00:37:02,881 --> 00:37:05,980
re-nourished a badly eroded stretch of beach.

575
00:37:05,981 --> 00:37:09,620
Manson Construction supplied the 230-ton capacity

576
00:37:09,621 --> 00:37:11,960
Derrick with a clamshell crane.

577
00:37:11,961 --> 00:37:14,160
And for five months, sand was scooped up

578
00:37:14,161 --> 00:37:17,360
from the bottom of the ocean and loaded onto barges.

579
00:37:17,361 --> 00:37:20,140
From there, the barges were tugged into position

580
00:37:20,141 --> 00:37:23,480
and unloaded into 40 feet of water offshore.

581
00:37:23,481 --> 00:37:26,440
Next, a 220-foot hydraulic dredge

582
00:37:26,441 --> 00:37:31,280
piped the sand onto the beach at a rate of 25,000 cubic yards a day.

583
00:37:32,100 --> 00:37:36,840
80% water and 20% sand, the slurry was then moved with bulldozers,

584
00:37:36,841 --> 00:37:40,180
which helped push the water out and shape the final beach.

585
00:37:40,820 --> 00:37:45,980
When it was finished, more than 420,000 tons of clean sand

586
00:37:45,981 --> 00:37:47,940
had been restored to the beach.

587
00:37:53,040 --> 00:37:56,060
But sometimes a beach can disappear completely.

588
00:37:56,440 --> 00:38:00,180
practically pulling the rug out from under seaside structures.

589
00:38:00,280 --> 00:38:02,340
Desperate situations like these call

590
00:38:02,440 --> 00:38:05,680
for a different kind of beach technology, especially

591
00:38:05,780 --> 00:38:08,580
in the case of a beloved national landmark.

592
00:38:13,520 --> 00:38:16,560
Since 1870, the Cape Hatteras Lighthouse

593
00:38:16,660 --> 00:38:19,900
was a beacon to countless ships trying to negotiate

594
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:22,740
the treacherous coast off North Carolina.

595
00:38:22,840 --> 00:38:25,440
But a century later, the lighthouse itself

596
00:38:25,441 --> 00:38:26,340
was in danger.

597
00:38:31,140 --> 00:38:33,980
They knew they were in trouble in about 1920.

598
00:38:34,080 --> 00:38:36,780
Of the original 1,600 feet to the ocean,

599
00:38:36,880 --> 00:38:40,180
there only remained about 300 feet to the shore in 1920.

600
00:38:40,280 --> 00:38:44,500
60 years later, the shore was down to 160 feet.

601
00:38:44,600 --> 00:38:47,260
So International Chimney Company was brought in

602
00:38:47,360 --> 00:38:50,240
to pick up the 4,800-ton lighthouse

603
00:38:50,340 --> 00:38:52,800
and move it to a location half a mile inland.

604
00:38:55,440 --> 00:39:04,820
In 1999, workers began unearthing the massive foundation, which was then replaced by a grid

605
00:39:04,821 --> 00:39:06,172
of steel beams.

606
00:39:06,173 --> 00:39:13,200
Next, 100 synchronized hydraulic jacks lifted it six feet and placed it onto a

607
00:39:13,201 --> 00:39:14,587
huge roller system.

608
00:39:14,588 --> 00:39:20,280
For 22 days, the Cape Hatteras Lighthouse slowly made its way to its new home.

609
00:39:20,281 --> 00:39:25,380
far away from the hazards of the unpredictable beach.

610
00:39:29,180 --> 00:39:31,380
Sometimes the landmark gets moved,

611
00:39:31,640 --> 00:39:33,400
and sometimes the beach does.

612
00:39:35,800 --> 00:39:39,060
At the Mandalay Bay Hotel in Las Vegas, Nevada,

613
00:39:39,420 --> 00:39:43,600
guests enjoy their very own synthetic shoreline with a twist.

614
00:39:43,601 --> 00:39:46,920
We have a beach in the middle of the desert here.

615
00:39:46,921 --> 00:39:52,260
We have 1,700 tons of sand, this is the beach area,

616
00:39:52,480 --> 00:39:55,160
and then 1.6 million gallons of water,

617
00:39:55,320 --> 00:39:57,000
which consists of the wave pull.

618
00:39:57,900 --> 00:40:00,960
Designed and built by Murphy's Waves of Scotland,

619
00:40:01,400 --> 00:40:05,340
this landlocked sea produces one breaker every 108 seconds.

620
00:40:06,000 --> 00:40:09,800
The system is capable of creating waves as high as 13 feet

621
00:40:09,801 --> 00:40:13,280
in a variety of configurations, but for safety reasons,

622
00:40:13,281 --> 00:40:16,960
They make them parallel to the shore and rarely over 3 1

623
00:40:16,961 --> 00:40:19,660
1⁄2 feet tall.

624
00:40:19,661 --> 00:40:23,620
There's 10 holding chambers that hold about 54,000 gallons

625
00:40:23,621 --> 00:40:24,460
of water.

626
00:40:24,461 --> 00:40:27,460
The water goes into the chambers, and then it pushes out

627
00:40:27,461 --> 00:40:29,460
through the gravity, and that's what makes the waves.

628
00:40:37,100 --> 00:40:40,040
Behind the enormous concrete facade,

629
00:40:40,041 --> 00:40:49,780
Five 150-horsepower turbine engines capable of pumping 850,000 gallons a minute fill the huge chambers with water.

630
00:40:56,620 --> 00:41:02,160
But lifting the massive three-ton gates that will unleash the wave is not easy.

631
00:41:02,740 --> 00:41:06,640
With about 70 tons of water pressure pushing against each gate,

632
00:41:06,641 --> 00:41:10,880
The hardest working part of this wave machine is the lifting system.

633
00:41:13,800 --> 00:41:19,480
Each unit must first build up enough pressure before it has the power to go into action.

634
00:41:29,480 --> 00:41:33,940
After the wave has broken, the water spills into the surrounding grains

635
00:41:33,941 --> 00:41:36,940
and goes back into the 27-foot-deep reservoir

636
00:41:36,941 --> 00:41:39,420
under the facility where it gets filtered.

637
00:41:40,160 --> 00:41:42,780
On the way, chlorine is automatically added,

638
00:41:43,100 --> 00:41:44,900
up to 1,500 gallons a week.

639
00:41:45,460 --> 00:41:48,200
It takes only three hours to completely clean

640
00:41:48,201 --> 00:41:49,800
and recycle the entire pool.

641
00:41:49,801 --> 00:41:54,060
But every 108 seconds, the machinery goes back to work

642
00:41:54,061 --> 00:41:56,180
so the people can have fun.

643
00:42:03,940 --> 00:42:16,100
Meanwhile, the ancient and natural machinery of sand and surf continues to chug along,

644
00:42:16,640 --> 00:42:18,660
as it has since our planet was young.

645
00:42:19,400 --> 00:42:25,560
And if indeed we did come from the ocean, then these sandy thresholds are where we,

646
00:42:25,940 --> 00:42:29,220
like Columbus, first encountered the rest of the world.

647
00:42:29,221 --> 00:42:32,220
Since then, we may have developed technologies

648
00:42:32,320 --> 00:42:34,620
that enhance our enjoyment of the beach,

649
00:42:34,720 --> 00:42:38,220
but it's doubtful we'll ever duplicate one perfectly.

650
00:42:38,320 --> 00:42:40,560
And maybe that's for the best.

651
00:42:40,660 --> 00:42:43,960
I prefer the living beach because I want my sandcastles

652
00:42:44,060 --> 00:42:47,080
to be built out of real sand.

653
00:42:47,180 --> 00:42:50,380
Technology can build a better beach

654
00:42:50,480 --> 00:42:56,580
if technology sets its mind to protecting, restoring,

655
00:42:56,581 --> 00:43:01,320
restoring, reviving those elements that have nourished

656
00:43:01,420 --> 00:43:03,920
our bodies and our souls and our spirits.

657
00:43:07,320 --> 00:43:09,920
If the past is any indication, technology

658
00:43:10,020 --> 00:43:14,240
will continue to make beaches safer, cleaner, and more

659
00:43:14,340 --> 00:43:19,940
hospitable to the people who leave their footprints on them.

